@page "/form"

<CatHead Title="Forms">
    <code>.form-field</code> stacks a label, a control and a hint with the right gaps.
    <code>.form-input</code> works on <code>input</code>, <code>select</code> and
    <code>textarea</code> alike — it is width-100%, so the parent controls the size.
    Every control here is a native element with a class on it: no wrappers, no
    <code>@@bind</code> to work around, and <code>required</code> /
    <code>aria-invalid</code> keep doing what they already do.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.Fields" Title="Fields" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>Label, control, hint. The hint explains the consequence, not the control.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.Selects" Title="Selects" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong><code>.form-select</code> styles the drop-down as well as the box.</strong> It sets
        <code>appearance: base-select</code>, which takes the option list out of the platform's hands
        and makes it a real panel in the page — so it takes the theme, the tokens and the
        <code>.menu</code> panel's own surface, border and shadow. Nothing has to be added to the
        markup: a plain <code>&lt;select class="form-select"&gt;</code> gets it.
    </p>
    <p>
        A browser without base appearance keeps the platform's list and gets a caret painted from two
        gradients instead — the stylesheet loads and inlines nothing, and without base appearance a
        pseudo-element does not render on a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> at all. Neither half is a
        script or a shim; the two are exclusive, behind <code>@@supports</code>. A list box
        (<code>multiple</code>, or <code>size</code> above 1) has no caret either way, since there is
        nothing to drop.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong><code>.form-select</code> is the whole class list a dropdown needs.</strong> It
        carries the same field shape <code>.form-input</code> does — height, padding, surface,
        border, radius, type and focus ring all come from one rule the two names share — so a
        select never has to be told twice what a field looks like.
        <code>class="form-input form-select"</code> resolves to those same declarations and stays
        correct; it is simply longer.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.SelectOptions" Title="Options with content" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        An <code>&lt;option&gt;</code> may now hold real markup — an icon, a second line, a trailing
        code. Add a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> holding
        <code>&lt;selectedcontent&gt;&lt;/selectedcontent&gt;</code> as the select's first child and the
        closed box shows a clone of whichever option is chosen.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Write the button.</strong> Leave it out and the browser supplies its own, which shows
        every text node in the option run together — an option carrying an
        <code>.option-hint</code> then reads <code>Frankfurteu-central-1</code> in the closed box, and
        the icon does not travel into it at all.
    </p>
    <p>
        The clone is the browser's to make, and <strong>Blazor's render does not trigger it</strong>:
        left alone the closed box is blank, with nothing in the console to say why.
        <code>Sedna.UI.js</code> repairs that on load and after every render, so this only matters if
        the script is not on the page — see <code>sednaUi.select.refresh()</code> on the
        <a href="/script">script page</a>. The rest of the control needs no JavaScript.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.option-hint</code> is the quiet half of a row, and it reads its own position: a direct
        child of the <code>&lt;option&gt;</code> is a trailing note, like a shortcut beside a menu
        item; inside <code>.option-text</code> it is a second line under the label. The clone in the
        closed box drops it either way, because the field is one row tall.
    </p>
    <p>
        Give a group a <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> as well as its <code>label</code>. The attribute's
        heading is drawn by the browser outside this document and cannot be themed — it is what a
        browser without base appearance shows, and the <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> is what this one
        does. An <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code> between groups is a separator like any other box.
    </p>
    <p>
        Keyboard, typeahead and the accessible name are the platform's, unchanged — this is still one
        <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> with no ARIA on it. Mark a decorative icon
        <code>aria-hidden="true"</code> so it is not announced twice, and remember that the submitted
        value is the option's text run together and trimmed: keep <code>value</code> explicit.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.TwoUpRows" Title="Two-up rows" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.field-grid</code> lays fields out side by side and collapses to one column when
        there is not room for two — no breakpoint to pick.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.Checkboxes" Title="Checkboxes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-check</code> is the clickable label wrapper — the whole label toggles the
        box. The check mark is drawn in CSS, so it picks up the brand token.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.RadioButtons" Title="Radio buttons" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same <code>.form-check</code> wrapper — the type attribute decides the shape. A radio
        is an unfilled circle with a brand dot rather than a filled disc, so shape
        <em>and</em> fill differ from a checkbox and which one is exclusive reads without
        colour. Give every radio in a set the same <code>name</code>, and wrap the set in a
        <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> so its label is announced with each option.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.Switches" Title="Switches" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.switch</code> is for a setting that takes effect the moment it is flipped. A
        checkbox is for a value saved with the rest of the form. The difference is when it
        applies, not how it looks — picking the wrong one misleads the reader about whether
        <strong>Save</strong> is still needed. It is still an
        <code>&lt;input type="checkbox"&gt;</code>, so it needs no ARIA.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.Sizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-input-sm</code>, the default, and <code>.form-input-lg</code>, matching
        <code>.btn-sm</code>, <code>.btn</code> and <code>.btn-lg</code> exactly. An input group takes
        the tier on the group with <code>.input-group--sm</code> or <code>--lg</code>, because the
        group is what draws the border.
    </p>
    <p>
        A select takes <code>.form-select-sm</code> or <code>.form-select-lg</code> — its own tier,
        not the input's, because a painted caret needs its room back at every size. A small text
        filter and a small dropdown beside it then agree on height, which is the row a toolbar is
        made of.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.Validation" Title="Validation" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The error border keys off <code>aria-invalid</code>, not a class. The attribute is what a
        screen reader announces, so styling a separate class would let the two disagree — and the
        reader who most needs the message is the one who would not get it. Point
        <code>aria-describedby</code> at the message so it is read as part of the field.
        <code>.form-warning</code> is for a value that is accepted but unwise.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.InputGroups" Title="Input groups" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        One border around a control and its affixes. The affixes are real siblings rather than
        padding plus a background image, so a button stays clickable and a unit stays selectable.
        The focus ring moves out to the group and the inner control gives up its own, or the group
        draws two rings nested inside each other.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.ReadOnlyValues" Title="Read-only values" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-value-display</code> is the shape of an input without being one — for
        values the user can read but not change. Do not use a disabled input for this; it reads
        as broken rather than as informational.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.SectionsActions" Title="Sections and actions" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-section</code> is a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> with the browser's border
        and inset legend removed. Use the real element: the legend is then announced with every
        control in the group, which a heading above a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> is not. The fields
        go in <code>.form-section-body</code> rather than in the fieldset directly, because a
        <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> is not a flex item and lands somewhere different in every
        engine if you make the fieldset the flex container.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.form-actions</code> closes the form. It wraps instead of overflowing, so three
        buttons on a phone stack rather than pushing the commit button off-screen.
        <code>.form-actions--split</code> puts a destructive action on the far side, where it
        cannot be hit by aiming for <strong>Save</strong>, and
        <code>.form-actions--start</code> aligns the row to the leading edge — for a form embedded
        in a page rather than closing a dialog, where the far corner is nowhere in particular.
    </p>
    <p>
        Where the group genuinely is not a set of related controls — a heading over a region rather
        than over fields — use <code>.form-section-title</code> on a plain element instead of a
        <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code>. It is the same rule, without claiming a grouping that is not
        there.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Form.SettingsCard" Title="A settings card" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>Fields in a card, actions in a footer row.</p>
</CatExample>
